  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nDense cumulus field across the region this afternoon as low level \nmoisture remains in place from yesterday evenings light rain \nactivity. Despite the increased cloud cover we are still looking at \nhighs in the 60s for the majority of the region. Expect this cloud \ncover to fade this evening. However, cloud cover will gradually \nincrease and thicken overnight from the south ahead of an \napproaching low pressure system, with overnight lows settling in the \nupper 30s to upper 40s. \n\nRain chances will increase early Friday morning as a low pressure \nsystem tracks out of the Plains towards the Midwest. Moisture will \noverspread the region from south to north, but drier air from the \ndeparting high pressure over Canada will create a sharp northern \nedge to the precipitation shield. The latest trend in the models \ncontinues to eat away at the rain totals with our southern tier of \ncounties now less likely to receive an inch of rain. Current \nprobabilities have dropped to 30-50% chance of at least an inches \nfrom the Brainerd Lakes region east into Price County. Areas farther \nnorth toward the Borderlands may see little to no measurable rain. \nAdditionally, instability with this passing system remains low so \nthunderstorms are not expected. Rain will persist into Saturday \nmorning before tapering off west to east. \n\nStrong northeasterly winds will develop Friday and remain enhanced \nthrough Saturday. The favorable direction will lead to rip current \nand building wave heights for the beaches of the Twin Ports. \n\nHigh pressure settles over the region for the late weekend period, \nbringing a return to dry weather for Sunday. Skies will clear out \nas cooler Canadian air overspreads the Northland, setting up ideal \nconditions for radiational cooling. Inland locations will see \ntemperatures fall into the mid and upper 30s Sunday and Monday \nnight, leading to the potential for patchy frost across low-lying \nareas. \n\nThe dry pattern will persist into early next week with mostly sunny \ndays and chilly nights. A gradual warming trend will take hold by \nWednesday, with high temperatures returning to seasonable values in \nthe mid 60s to low 70s. Rain chances may return by late Wednesday \nnight into Thursday, though confidence remains low.",
